.TH CBMAP_CONSTRUCT_CC 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cbmap_construct_cc \- declare and create an ordered map ordered by a custom comparison procedure
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap_construct_cc(hm_name, key_t, val_t, custom_comparison_proc);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares an ordered map handle named hm_name with the companion type
variables that carry key_t and val_t to every later type-inferred macro,
then creates a map whose keys are ordered by custom_comparison_proc
instead of the default order for key_t.

Think of it as cbmap_construct(3) with a comparison procedure added; the
allocator is the default one. cbmap_construct_mp(3) replaces the
allocator instead, and cbmap_construct_full(3) takes both.
cbmap_construct_cc_scoped(3) is the same macro with a cleanup attribute
that destroys the map when the scope exits.

A struct key_t needs this variant. Without a procedure the map orders a
key type that it doesn't recognize by a raw comparison of the key's
representation, which reads the padding bytes and orders a pointer member
by its address.

The two-step form is cbmap_declare(3) followed by cbmap_init_cc(3). Use it
when the caller has to handle a creation failure and that failure must
not be fatal.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name                 Name for the map handle to declare
key_t                   Key type. Every later macro reads a key through
                        it, and the container takes from it the width to
                        expect and the alignment of a stored key
val_t                   Value type. Only the type-inferred macros read
                        it; the library never passes it to the container
custom_comparison_proc  A ccol_comparison_proc_t, or NULL for the
                        default order for key_t
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. The macro declares variables and doesn't expand to an expression.
.fi
.SH "CUSTOM COMPARISON"
.nf
The procedure receives pointers to two keys. It returns a negative value
when the first key orders before the second, a positive value when it
orders after the second, and zero when the map must treat the two as the
same key. Since a zero turns an insert into an update, the procedure
defines key equality as well as key order.

The procedure must give a strict weak order over key_t, and that order
must not change while the map holds elements: the same pair of keys
always compares the same way, swapping the two gives the opposite sign,
and the order is transitive. An inconsistent procedure breaks the
placement invariant of the tree, and after that the map can't find keys
that it holds.

The procedure gets two bare pointers and no lengths, so it can only read
a width that key_t fixes. That is why the map checks the width of an
incoming key against key_t and aligns its stored keys for key_t; the
procedure decides the order of the keys and nothing else.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro stops the program through _ccol_fatal_err() if the creation
fails, and it uses the default allocator.

The procedure must stay callable for the whole life of the map.

A walk visits the elements in ascending key order, by the order of this
procedure; cbmap_begin_iter(3) describes that contract.

Destroy the map with cbmap_destroy(3). The companion type variables
follow the scope of the handle, so a function that receives the handle
has to call cbmap_redeclare(3) first.

An array key type or value type, such as int[2] or char[16], is a compile
error whose message says to wrap the array in a struct. The macros copy
keys and values by assignment and C can't assign an array, so the map
would otherwise store the address of the caller's array instead of its
elements. A struct that holds the array works.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
typedef struct { int major; int minor; } version;

int version_cmp(const void *a, const void *b) {
    version x, y;
    memcpy(&x, a, sizeof(x));   /* a key can be at any address */
    memcpy(&y, b, sizeof(y));
    if (x.major != y.major) {
        return (x.major > y.major) - (x.major < y.major);
    }
    return (x.minor > y.minor) - (x.minor < y.minor);
}

cbmap_construct_cc(releases, version, char *, version_cmp);
cbmap_destroy(releases);
.fi
